WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.641
Welcome to Module 7, the last module of
the HR-Driven Identity learning path:

2
00:00:05.641 --> 00:00:08.888
find lifecycle drift
and file the evidence.

3
00:00:08.888 --> 00:00:11.767
This is Gate 7 of the
implementation guide,

4
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:15.387
and it goes with Chapter 07
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:15.387 --> 00:00:18.162
The first six gates each produce a result.

6
00:00:18.162 --> 00:00:21.084
This gate keeps those
results true over time,

7
00:00:21.084 --> 00:00:23.926
and hands the ISSO
something they can check.

8
00:00:23.926 --> 00:00:29.235
In this module you'll write one read-only
run of the lifecycle drift classes and the

9
00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:32.827
six lifecycle metrics, from
the HR export, the directory,

10
00:00:32.827 --> 00:00:35.950
the card system and the
earlier modules' outputs.

11
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.898
Then you'll package each
run with a hash chain,

12
00:00:38.898 --> 00:00:41.687
verify it, and assemble
a reporting period.

13
00:00:41.687 --> 00:00:44.530
The module takes about
an hour with its lab.

14
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.768
Why does this gate exist?

15
00:00:49.768 --> 00:00:52.584
Because accounts drift from the HR record,

16
00:00:52.584 --> 00:00:55.481
even after every earlier
gate is done right.

17
00:00:55.481 --> 00:00:57.967
A worker shows up in HR with no account.

18
00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.768
A terminated worker's
account is still enabled.

19
00:01:00.768 --> 00:01:03.079
An account turns up that nobody owns.

20
00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:05.993
Someone other than the named
writer changes a value.

21
00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:08.405
A card stays active after a separation.

22
00:01:08.405 --> 00:01:12.717
Each of those is a drift class, and
the run in this module finds them all.

23
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:14.805
There's a second problem: time.

24
00:01:14.805 --> 00:01:18.771
Entra keeps its own logs for only
thirty days, and the evidence has

25
00:01:18.771 --> 00:01:23.105
to outlive the accounts it describes,
which are eventually disposed of.

26
00:01:23.105 --> 00:01:26.906
So the findings and metrics
have to leave the directory,

27
00:01:26.906 --> 00:01:29.430
in a form an ISSO can check later.

28
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.355
Here's what you'll be
able to do by the end.

29
00:01:35.355 --> 00:01:39.316
You'll name the six lifecycle
drift classes and the six metrics,

30
00:01:39.316 --> 00:01:41.830
and say which module's output feeds each.

31
00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.055
You'll write a run from the earlier
modules' outputs and read its findings.

32
00:01:46.055 --> 00:01:48.555
You'll package runs with a hash chain,

33
00:01:48.555 --> 00:01:52.305
verify a package, and check
it yourself with sha256sum.

34
00:01:52.305 --> 00:01:54.601
And you'll assemble a reporting period,

35
00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:58.405
and explain why it can be incomplete
even when its chain is whole.

36
00:01:58.405 --> 00:02:04.255
This run reads the outputs
of Modules 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6.

37
00:02:04.255 --> 00:02:07.867
You don't need to have kept them: the
first step of the lab rebuilds them.

38
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:12.008
The packaging builds on the drift
pipeline in Chapter 09 of the Federal

39
00:02:12.008 --> 00:02:16.008
Modernization Workbook's Building
the Attribute-Driven Migration,

40
00:02:16.008 --> 00:02:18.620
and the lab calls it from the same clone.

41
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.458
Unit 1 reads the guide's Step
7, which asks for five things.

42
00:02:26.458 --> 00:02:32.495
Run the drift classes: that's the run
command, with classes L D one to L D six.

43
00:02:32.495 --> 00:02:37.080
Fix at the source, not in the directory:
the period counts findings opened

44
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.970
and closed, and keeps each unauthorized-write
finding's closure at the source.

45
00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.517
Report the six metrics:

46
00:02:43.517 --> 00:02:47.832
the run computes them and flags
any out of bounds or not measured.

47
00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:52.819
Prove the sole source for each
population: this code doesn't decide that;

48
00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:56.877
it's seven days of provisioning
logs against the audit log,

49
00:02:56.877 --> 00:02:59.920
in Appendix C of the
HR-Driven Identity book.

50
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.948
And keep the evidence
outside the directory:

51
00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:06.058
that's the package, verify
and period commands.

52
00:03:06.058 --> 00:03:10.294
Chapter 11 of the HR-Driven
Identity book has the design.

53
00:03:10.294 --> 00:03:12.644
Attribute drift isn't this module's;

54
00:03:12.644 --> 00:03:17.050
it belongs to Gate 9 of the
attribute-driven migration guide.

55
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.188
Unit 2 turns to the workbook chapter.

56
00:03:23.188 --> 00:03:24.476
Here are the classes.

57
00:03:24.476 --> 00:03:29.075
L D one, missing identity: an active
worker record with no account.

58
00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:34.559
L D two, stale access: a worker terminated
in HR whose account is still enabled,

59
00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:38.563
or a contractor past the
earliest end and still enabled.

60
00:03:38.563 --> 00:03:40.291
L D three, orphan account:

61
00:03:40.291 --> 00:03:44.874
an enabled account that matches
no worker or contractor record.

62
00:03:44.874 --> 00:03:46.887
L D four, unauthorized write:

63
00:03:46.887 --> 00:03:51.437
Module 1's audit scan found a
second writer or a forbidden write.

64
00:03:51.437 --> 00:03:56.638
L D five, credential drift: an active
PIV card for a separated worker.

65
00:03:56.638 --> 00:04:00.906
And L D six, the contractor version:
an active card for someone whose

66
00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:05.175
engagement ended, or whom the
contractor source doesn't hold at all.

67
00:04:05.175 --> 00:04:06.663
Those six are Findings.

68
00:04:06.663 --> 00:04:10.105
Two more rules are warnings
about the metrics:

69
00:04:10.105 --> 00:04:13.990
L D seven, out of bounds,
and L D eight, not measured.

70
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:19.015
The six metrics.

71
00:04:19.015 --> 00:04:23.774
Two come from Module 6: the ninetieth
percentiles of separation-to-block,

72
00:04:23.774 --> 00:04:28.215
at most four hours, and
separation-to-revocation, at most twenty-four.

73
00:04:28.215 --> 00:04:31.688
Transfer-to-review comes from
Module 4's mover review,

74
00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:33.740
at most two hundred forty hours.

75
00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.628
The joiner readiness rate comes from
Module 3, at least ninety-five percent.

76
00:04:38.628 --> 00:04:42.003
And two are computed by this
run: correlation coverage,

77
00:04:42.003 --> 00:04:46.803
the share of enabled accounts that
resolve to a current worker or contractor,

78
00:04:46.803 --> 00:04:50.028
at least ninety-nine percent;
and derivation purity,

79
00:04:50.028 --> 00:04:55.203
the share of access-granting memberships
computed by rule, at least eighty percent.

80
00:04:55.203 --> 00:05:01.015
Module 1's scan and Module 2's contractor
decisions feed the drift classes instead.

81
00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:06.540
One rule matters most here: a metric with
no input is not measured, never zero.

82
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.478
And the thresholds are the
program's starting values.

83
00:05:09.478 --> 00:05:13.650
Choosing them is the agency's
decision under C A seven.

84
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.150
A run is one folder, and it only reads.

85
00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:25.730
Its run dot JSON records the tenant,
the cloud and the mode, and a SHA-256 of

86
00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:31.675
each module output it read, so anyone can
tell exactly which inputs produced it.

87
00:05:31.675 --> 00:05:36.938
Beside it, the lifecycle folder holds
the findings, the metrics and a manifest.

88
00:05:36.938 --> 00:05:41.354
Before the run reads an earlier
module's output, it checks that output

89
00:05:41.354 --> 00:05:45.849
against its own manifest, so a changed
file is caught, not silently used.

90
00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:48.771
The tools also write
each output folder once:

91
00:05:48.771 --> 00:05:52.087
they refuse a folder that
already holds a manifest.

92
00:05:52.087 --> 00:05:54.648
That's why the lab's
first step deletes the

93
00:05:54.648 --> 00:05:57.662
out folder and rebuilds
the five module outputs.

94
00:05:57.662 --> 00:06:00.325
Lab 1 writes its runs in fixture mode.

95
00:06:00.325 --> 00:06:05.030
Lab 2 writes them in tenant mode,
from your test tenant's exports.

96
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.892
The packaging is three commands
in the drift pipeline.

97
00:06:11.892 --> 00:06:16.504
Package seals one run: a SHA-256
for every file, the tenant I D,

98
00:06:16.504 --> 00:06:22.268
and the hash of the previous package's
manifest, which is what chains them.

99
00:06:22.268 --> 00:06:26.905
A package is written once, and a run
from a different tenant is refused.

100
00:06:26.905 --> 00:06:28.843
Verify rechecks a package.

101
00:06:28.843 --> 00:06:33.515
But the ISSO doesn't need the
kit to trust it: sha256sum,

102
00:06:33.515 --> 00:06:38.931
checking the SHA256SUMS file,
is their own independent check.

103
00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:42.193
Period assembles the packages
in a reporting window.

104
00:06:42.193 --> 00:06:45.618
If there's a gap in coverage
or a broken link in the chain,

105
00:06:45.618 --> 00:06:47.217
the period isn't complete.

106
00:06:47.217 --> 00:06:52.879
It also scores Gate 7's criteria and maps
the findings and metrics to five controls.

107
00:06:52.879 --> 00:06:58.330
That mapping is the program's design
guidance; the assessor decides each control.

108
00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:02.805
Try it.

109
00:07:02.805 --> 00:07:08.873
Step one deletes any out folder, then
rebuilds five inputs, one command each:

110
00:07:08.873 --> 00:07:14.744
Module 1's writer scan, Module 2's
contractor extract, Module 3's readiness,

111
00:07:14.744 --> 00:07:19.442
Module 4's mover residual and
Module 6's separation timing.

112
00:07:19.442 --> 00:07:22.200
The full commands are on the module page;

113
00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:26.043
each one exits two, because
each fixture holds findings.

114
00:07:26.043 --> 00:07:29.242
Step two writes the
first run, for October.

115
00:07:29.242 --> 00:07:33.054
It reads four module outputs
and prints the six metrics.

116
00:07:33.054 --> 00:07:36.333
Separation-to-block at
five point five two hours,

117
00:07:36.333 --> 00:07:39.693
and revocation at a hundred
ten, both out of bounds.

118
00:07:39.693 --> 00:07:44.480
Transfer-to-review isn't measured,
because the mover's review is still open.

119
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.483
Readiness is twenty-five percent,
correlation coverage fifty-eight percent,

120
00:07:49.483 --> 00:07:53.392
and derivation purity seventy-six
percent: all out of bounds.

121
00:07:53.392 --> 00:07:56.140
Fourteen Findings, six Warns.

122
00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.290
Step three prints the
findings, one line each.

123
00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.965
There are fourteen Findings.

124
00:08:04.965 --> 00:08:07.915
One missing identity, a
worker with no account.

125
00:08:07.915 --> 00:08:13.064
Three stale accounts, among them Drew
and Harper, two of Module 6's leavers,

126
00:08:13.064 --> 00:08:16.840
still enabled, and a contractor
past the earliest end.

127
00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:20.353
Two orphans, a print kiosk
and an old temp account.

128
00:08:20.353 --> 00:08:23.619
Five unauthorized writes,
which are Module 1's four

129
00:08:23.619 --> 00:08:27.352
second writers and its forbidden
delete, carried forward.

130
00:08:27.352 --> 00:08:29.572
One active card for a separated worker,

131
00:08:29.572 --> 00:08:32.139
and two contractor cards
that should be gone.

132
00:08:32.139 --> 00:08:36.834
Then six Warns: five L D seven,
one for each metric out of bounds,

133
00:08:36.834 --> 00:08:40.777
and one L D eight, for the
metric that wasn't measured.

134
00:08:40.777 --> 00:08:44.078
Notice how the earlier modules'
results reappear here.

135
00:08:44.078 --> 00:08:46.360
This run is where they all meet.

136
00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.901
Step four writes the next day's
run, for the first of November,

137
00:08:53.901 --> 00:08:57.073
and it prints the same
metrics and the same counts.

138
00:08:57.073 --> 00:08:59.461
Step five packages both runs.

139
00:08:59.461 --> 00:09:04.747
The first package opens twenty findings,
the fourteen Findings and six Warns.

140
00:09:04.747 --> 00:09:07.146
The second package names the first one's

141
00:09:07.146 --> 00:09:09.761
manifest as its previous,
which chains them.

142
00:09:09.761 --> 00:09:12.278
It opens nothing new and closes nothing,

143
00:09:12.278 --> 00:09:15.635
because the inputs didn't
change between the two runs.

144
00:09:15.635 --> 00:09:18.952
Then verify confirms the
second package: six files,

145
00:09:18.952 --> 00:09:22.585
and the same manifest hash
the package command printed.

146
00:09:22.585 --> 00:09:26.397
Your hashes match these only if your
fixtures are the committed ones.

147
00:09:26.397 --> 00:09:31.249
Finally, the ISSO's own check,
with no kit at all: sha256sum,

148
00:09:31.249 --> 00:09:36.150
run inside the package
folder, reports every file OK.

149
00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:45.175
Step six assembles the period, from the
first of October to the second of November.

150
00:09:45.175 --> 00:09:48.563
Two packages, no chain
breaks, no coverage gaps,

151
00:09:48.563 --> 00:09:52.487
twenty findings open at the
end, and complete is false.

152
00:09:52.487 --> 00:09:53.737
Exit code two.

153
00:09:53.737 --> 00:09:56.250
Print the period summary and you see why.

154
00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.425
It scores Gate 7's criteria.

155
00:09:58.425 --> 00:10:03.925
The evidence criterion is met: kept outside
the directory, with an unbroken chain.

156
00:10:03.925 --> 00:10:07.498
But drift is still open, so
the first criterion isn't met,

157
00:10:07.498 --> 00:10:11.388
and five metrics are out of
bounds, so the second isn't either.

158
00:10:11.388 --> 00:10:15.425
The fourth, the sole source,
isn't decided by this code at all.

159
00:10:15.425 --> 00:10:18.878
That's the point of this
step: an unbroken chain proves

160
00:10:18.878 --> 00:10:21.938
the record is intact, not
that the gate is passed.

161
00:10:21.938 --> 00:10:24.247
Step seven runs both test suites,

162
00:10:24.247 --> 00:10:29.400
fifty-seven and a hundred forty-two
tests, and deletes the out folder.

163
00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.772
Lab 2 runs the same thing for real,

164
00:10:35.772 --> 00:10:39.850
in a worldwide or GCC test
tenant, never production.

165
00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.578
The reader app uses a certificate
or a federated credential,

166
00:10:43.578 --> 00:10:48.500
and holds only the two permissions the
drift-read task lists, with admin consent.

167
00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:52.269
You export the workforce users,
run Lab 2 of Modules 1 to

168
00:10:52.269 --> 00:10:56.037
6 against the test tenant, and
write a run in tenant mode.

169
00:10:56.037 --> 00:11:00.845
Then you schedule it in the Chapter 09
workflow from Building the Attribute-Driven

170
00:11:00.845 --> 00:11:05.303
Migration, with each package written to
an immutable test storage container,

171
00:11:05.303 --> 00:11:07.950
and assemble a period
from the scheduled runs.

172
00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:11.310
To back it out, remove the
schedule, delete the reader app,

173
00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.817
its credential and its consent,
delete the test container

174
00:11:14.817 --> 00:11:18.250
if you created it for the lab,
and delete the local folders.

175
00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:20.750
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

176
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:25.887
The federal requirement.

177
00:11:25.887 --> 00:11:30.224
In NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three terms,

178
00:11:30.224 --> 00:11:36.006
this gate supports C A seven, continuous
monitoring with system-level metrics, and

179
00:11:36.006 --> 00:11:41.363
A U six and A U eleven with S I twelve:
the review and retention of audit records.

180
00:11:41.363 --> 00:11:45.975
It also supports BOD 25-01's
continuous reporting.

181
00:11:45.975 --> 00:11:50.150
Your organization still sets the
organization-defined parameters,

182
00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:53.300
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

183
00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:58.029
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 7: every period's manifest,

184
00:11:58.029 --> 00:12:02.475
summary and checksum file,
with the approved thresholds.

185
00:12:02.475 --> 00:12:07.250
The packages, with each unauthorized-write
finding's closure at the source.

186
00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.977
The storage container's retention
and immutability settings,

187
00:12:10.977 --> 00:12:12.625
set to the records schedule.

188
00:12:12.625 --> 00:12:15.250
And the ScubaGear results
beside the period.

189
00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:21.030
The guide's Evidence section gathers all
seven gates into one file per population.

190
00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:28.280
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

191
00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:31.442
First: Module 4's mover
review is still open,

192
00:12:31.442 --> 00:12:34.243
so there's no transfer-to-review value.

193
00:12:34.243 --> 00:12:35.730
How does the run report it?

194
00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:41.168
The answer is c: as not measured,
rule L D eight, never as zero.

195
00:12:41.168 --> 00:12:45.168
A zero would look like the best
possible result, and it would be false.

196
00:12:45.168 --> 00:12:48.412
Second: the period
reports zero chain breaks

197
00:12:48.412 --> 00:12:51.655
and zero coverage gaps,
but complete is false.

198
00:12:51.655 --> 00:12:52.343
Why?

199
00:12:52.343 --> 00:12:53.383
The answer is a.

200
00:12:53.383 --> 00:12:58.931
Drift is still open and metrics are out of
bounds, so Gate 7's criteria aren't met.

201
00:12:58.931 --> 00:13:00.991
The period scores the criteria,

202
00:13:00.991 --> 00:13:04.717
and an unbroken chain meets
only the evidence criterion.

203
00:13:04.717 --> 00:13:07.150
The module page has three more questions,

204
00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:10.010
each linked to the
section that teaches it.

205
00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:14.585
To sum up.

206
00:13:14.585 --> 00:13:17.225
You rebuilt the earlier modules' outputs,

207
00:13:17.225 --> 00:13:21.572
and wrote two daily runs of the
lifecycle drift classes and metrics.

208
00:13:21.572 --> 00:13:23.894
You packaged them with a hash chain,

209
00:13:23.894 --> 00:13:28.777
verified a package with the kit and
with sha256sum, and assembled a period

210
00:13:28.777 --> 00:13:33.260
that shows exactly which of Gate 7's
criteria are met, and which aren't.

211
00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:37.560
And with that, you've finished the
HR-Driven Identity learning path.

212
00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.793
Across seven modules you named one
writer for every account state,

213
00:13:41.793 --> 00:13:46.719
ran contractors from their own source,
made joiners ready on day one, assigned

214
00:13:46.719 --> 00:13:49.566
access from attributes,
checked card sign-in,

215
00:13:49.566 --> 00:13:52.260
timed separations, and filed the evidence.

216
00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:53.845
Go back to the learning path,

217
00:13:53.845 --> 00:13:57.410
or check your agency's progress
against the guide's checklist.
